D = {
 "Arcane Blight|IDROTF": "A humanoid spending 12 hours in the necropolis must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution save or catch this disease. Victims suffer hallucinations and paranoia, and a player character gains the flaw \"I don't trust anyone.\" After each long rest, the victim repeats the save. A success lowers the DC by 1d6, and at DC 0 the victim is cured and immune. Three failed saves turn the victim into a nothic, which only wish or divine intervention undoes. Greater restoration ends the infection but doesn't prevent reinfection.",
 "Blinding Sickness|PHB": "Pain grips the mind and the eyes turn milky white. The creature is blinded and has disadvantage on Wisdom checks and Wisdom saving throws. The contagion spell can inflict this disease.",
 "Blue Mist Fever|TOA": "A thin, odorless blue mist in the jungles of Chult infects giants and humanoids. Dispel magic destroys the mist in a 20-foot square. A typical encounter covers 1d6 such areas. A creature touching the mist must succeed on a DC 13 Constitution save or be infected. 1d6 hours after a failed save, the creature sees vivid hallucinations of blue monkeys until the disease ends. It repeats the save every 24 hours, and a success ends the disease.",
 "Bluerot|GOS": "Humanoids catch this disease, usually from wounds dealt by infected undead. Boils appear within 1d4 hours. The victim's Constitution and Charisma each drop by 1d4 (minimum 3). It becomes vulnerable to radiant damage and can breathe underwater. After each long rest, it makes a DC 12 Constitution save. A success restores 1 point of each lost score, and restoring all cures it. A failure deals 18 (4d8) necrotic damage. A creature dropped to 0 hit points this way can't regain hit points until cured but can be stabilized.",
 "Cackle Fever|DMG": "Humanoids catch this, but gnomes are immune. Symptoms start in 1d4 hours. The victim gains one level of exhaustion until cured. Stress forces a DC 13 Constitution save. On a failure, it takes 5 (1d10) psychic damage and is incapacitated by laughter for 1 minute, saving again each turn. Humanoids starting turns within 10 feet must succeed on a DC 10 Constitution save or catch it. After each long rest the victim saves again. A success lowers the DC by 1d6, and at DC 0 it recovers.",
 "Cackle Fever|XDMG": "Tainted potions spread this. Gnomes are immune. After 1d4 days the creature gains 1 Exhaustion level until the contagion ends. While exhausted, non-Psychic damage forces a DC 13 Constitution save. On a failure, it takes 5 (1d10) Psychic damage and is Incapacitated, saving each turn and passing after 1 minute. After each Long Rest it saves again. Three successes end it and grant 1 year of immunity. A Humanoid starting a turn within 10 feet of a carrier must succeed on a DC 10 Constitution save or catch it.",
 "Filth Fever|PHB": "A raging fever sweeps through the body. The creature has disadvantage on Strength checks, Strength saving throws and attack rolls that use Strength. The contagion spell can inflict this disease.",
 "Flesh Rot|PHB": "The creature's flesh decays. It has disadvantage on Charisma checks and is vulnerable to all damage. The contagion spell can inflict this disease.",
 "Frigid Woe|EGW": "Only a special antidote cures this disease, a milky liquid in gold vials in the ruins of Eiselcross. Drinking it takes an action and ends all effects. Blue spores spread it. A creature touching them must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution save or be infected. Symptoms appear after 1d4 days. The victim's speed drops by 5 feet. Every 10 days after, it must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution save or lose another 5 feet. At speed 0 it dies and becomes an ice statue.",
 "Grackle-Lung|OOTA": "Smog in Gracklstugh causes this disease. Each time a living, breathing creature finishes a long rest there, it makes a DC 11 Constitution save. Each failure adds a level of exhaustion, and level 6 kills. While exhausted by it, the creature needs a Constitution check to Dash or finish a spell with a verbal component, or it fails. The DC is 10 plus its exhaustion level. Below level 1 the disease ends, with a week of immunity. Duergar and derro have advantage on the checks. Any cure for disease works.",
 "Mindfire|PHB": "The creature's mind turns feverish. It has disadvantage on Intelligence checks and Intelligence saving throws. In combat it behaves as if under the confusion spell. The contagion spell can inflict this disease.",
 "Redface|GOS": "Redface has the same effects as sight rot from the Dungeon Master's Guide. Polluted air causes it instead of tainted water, so it is hard to avoid.",
 "Saprophytic Plague|CM": "A beast or humanoid within 10 feet of an infected creature must succeed on a DC 20 Constitution save or be infected. Success gives 24 hours of immunity. Symptoms start 1d4 hours later with a level of exhaustion, then one more every 24 hours. After 24 hours the victim acts like a zombie, grows fungus and tries to spread the plague. Victims who die become magenta ooze. Exposed food rots in 2d12 hours, and eating it needs the same save. Any cure for disease works.",
 "Seizure|PHB": "The creature shakes uncontrollably. It has disadvantage on Dexterity checks, Dexterity saving throws and attack rolls that use Dexterity. The contagion spell can inflict this disease.",
 "Sewer Plague|DMG": "Sewers, refuse heaps and swamps breed this disease. A humanoid bitten by a carrier or touching contaminated filth must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution save or be infected. Symptoms appear after 1d4 days. The victim gains one level of exhaustion, regains only half the usual hit points from Hit Dice, and regains none from a long rest. After each long rest it makes a DC 11 Constitution save. A failure adds a level of exhaustion. A success removes one. Dropping below level 1 cures the disease.",
 "Sewer Plague|XDMG": "Fouled potions and alchemical waste breed this contagion. A Humanoid wounded by a carrier or touching contaminated filth must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution save or be infected. 1d4 days later the creature gains 1 Exhaustion level. While exhausted, it regains only half the Hit Points from Hit Point Dice, and a Long Rest restores no Hit Points and removes no Exhaustion. Each dawn it makes a DC 11 Constitution save. A failure adds 1 Exhaustion level. A success removes 1. At 0 levels the contagion ends.",
 "Shivering Sickness|TOA": "Chult insects carry this. Repellent protects. A giant or humanoid damaged by a swarm of insects, giant centipede, giant scorpion or giant wasp is exposed after the encounter. Without repellent since its last long rest, it is exposed after a long rest. Exposure needs a DC 11 Constitution save, with advantage for natural armor. Symptoms appear after 2d6 hours. The victim regains half the usual hit points from Hit Dice, none from a long rest, and has disadvantage on checks and attacks. It saves after each long rest.",
 "Sight Rot|DMG": "A beast or humanoid drinking water tainted by sight rot must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution save or be infected. After one day, vision blurs, giving a -1 penalty to attack rolls and ability checks that rely on sight. The penalty grows by 1 after each long rest. At -5 the victim is blind until lesser restoration, heal or similar magic restores sight. The swamp flower Eyebright cures it. An herbalism kit makes one ointment dose per hour. One dose halts worsening after a rest, and three cure it.",
 "Sight Rot|XDMG": "A Beast or Humanoid drinking water tainted by Sight Rot must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution save or be Blinded until the contagion ends. Heal or Lesser Restoration ends it at once. With an Herbalism Kit, proficiency makes one ointment dose in 1 hour. Applied to the eyes, it suppresses the contagion for 24 hours. Three doses, covering 72 hours, end it. A Humanoid touching an infected creature's skin must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution save or be infected. Success gives 24 hours of immunity to that carrier.",
 "Slimy Doom|PHB": "The creature bleeds uncontrollably. It has disadvantage on Constitution checks and Constitution saving throws. Whenever it takes damage, it is stunned until the end of its next turn. The contagion spell can inflict this disease.",
 "Spider Eggs|WDMM": "If the party is defeated, stabilized characters wake after 1d4 hours cocooned in webs 10 feet off the floor with spider eggs inside them. A cocooned character is restrained and can use an action to try to break free with a DC 15 Strength check. The eggs hatch in 2d12 hours and kill the host. This counts as a disease. Lesser restoration or similar magic on the host kills the eggs.",
 "Super-Tetanus|TFTYP": "A creature with super-tetanus has a racing heart and muscle spasms strong enough to break bones. It takes 11 (2d10) damage at the start of each of its turns. If nothing else cures it, the victim repeats its saving throw at the end of every minute after exposure, and a success ends the effect.",
 "The Gnawing Plague|VRGR": "Bites from carrier rats or contact with an infected creature expose a creature to the Gnaws. It must succeed on a DC 10 Constitution save or be infected. The DC may rise as the plague spreads. Symptoms appear after 1d2 days. The victim gains 1 level of exhaustion, regains half the usual hit points from Hit Dice, and none from a long rest. After each long rest it saves at DC 10. Failure adds a level of exhaustion and success removes one. Below level 1 it recovers. Rats are immune.",
 "Throat Leeches|TOA": "Tiny parasites infest water in Chult. A giant or humanoid that swallows tainted water must succeed on a DC 12 Constitution save or be infested. After 1d6 hours, the victim gains 1 level of exhaustion that stays until the cure. After each long rest it repeats the save. A failure adds a level of exhaustion. A success removes one. Dropping below level 1 cures it. Drinking only rainwater, boiled water or magically purified water prevents it.",
 "Bloodied|XPHB": "A creature is Bloodied while it has half its Hit Points or fewer.",
 "Concentration|PHB": "Some spells need concentration to stay active. If you lose it, the spell ends. You can end it any time with no action. Normal activity doesn't interfere. Casting another concentration spell ends it. When you take damage, make a Constitution save with DC 10 or half the damage, whichever is higher. Each damage source needs its own save. Being incapacitated or dying ends it. The DM may call for a DC 10 Constitution save for hazards such as a wave crashing over a ship.",
 "Concentration|XPHB": "Some spells and effects need Concentration to stay active. If the creator loses it, the effect ends. The creator can end it at any time with no action. Starting a spell or effect that needs Concentration ends the previous one. Taking damage requires a Constitution save with DC 10 or half the damage (round down), whichever is higher, to a maximum of DC 30. The Incapacitated condition or death ends it.",
 "Surprised|PHB": "When one side ambushes the other, it gains surprise. The DM compares Dexterity (Stealth) checks of hiders with the passive Wisdom (Perception) of the other side. If neither side hides, they notice each other. Anyone who doesn't notice a threat is surprised at the start of the encounter. A surprised creature can't move or take an action on its first turn and can't take a reaction until that turn ends. Some members of a group can be surprised while others aren't.",
 "Surprised|XPHB": "A creature caught unaware at the start of combat is surprised and has disadvantage on its Initiative roll.",
 "Ghoul Gut|OOW": "Water polluted with ghoul parts carries this. Drinking it requires a DC 12 Constitution save or infection. Symptoms hit when the victim starts a long rest or gets excited, as in combat. The first time, it takes 5 (2d4) necrotic damage. The victim regains no hit points or Hit Dice from nonmagical sources. After a long rest, a DC 12 Constitution save ends it, and failure deals another 5 (2d4) necrotic damage. A caregiver who forgoes its own long rest benefit to tend the victim can cure it with a DC 15 Wisdom (Medicine) check.",
 "Lichen Plague|FRAIF": "A Beast, Giant, Humanoid or Monstrosity touching bile lichen spores risks this. After 1d4 days, lichen appears. After each Long Rest, the creature must succeed on a DC 14 Constitution save or gain 1 Exhaustion level that lasts until the contagion ends. A Humanoid dying infected rises as a Zombie under DM control. Three successful saves, not necessarily consecutive, end it with permanent immunity. Touching the lichen requires a DC 14 Constitution save or infection, with 24 hours of immunity on a success.",
 "Mudpox|FRAIF": "Mud tainted by dead Underdark oozes spreads this. A Beast or Humanoid touching it must succeed on a DC 12 Constitution save or be infected. Each dawn, the creature must succeed on a DC 12 Constitution save or vomit its previous day's food and gain 1 Exhaustion level. Three consecutive successes end it. Heal or Lesser Restoration ends it at once. A Humanoid touching an infected creature's skin must succeed on a DC 12 Constitution save or be infected, with 24 hours of immunity to that carrier on a success.",
 "Shaking Plague|FRAIF": "Failed magical experiments created this Humanoid plague. After 1d4 days the creature gains 1 Exhaustion level, and Long Rests can't reduce exhaustion while it has any. It has pus boils and disadvantage on Dexterity checks and saves. Casting a Somatic spell requires a DC 11 Constitution save or the spell fails, wasting the action but not the slot. Each dawn it saves at DC 11, gaining a level on a failure and losing one on a success. At 0 it ends. Skin contact requires a DC 11 save.",
 "The Rusting|FRAIF": "An iron coat grows over the victim and rusts when wet. Anyone wounded by a Rusted creature, or spending 1 hour where the Rusting has spread, makes a DC 10 Constitution save. Constructs and Undead pass. On a failure, the creature gains 1 Exhaustion level and is cursed, so Long Rests don't reduce exhaustion, and its Speed drops by 5 feet. At level 6 it becomes a Rusted Construct instead of dying, immune to poison, Exhaustion, Petrified and Poisoned. Each dawn it saves at DC 10, gaining or losing a level. Removing the curse ends it.",
}
